From e52f580fe97fb3262696fc8b03487a86a4eab2f4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Devesh Rai <devesh.rai@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 15:20:04 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] android: a frame and a title bar for dialogs

There is no window manager for the windows inside an Android activity,
so a dialog was drawn as a bare rectangle: no frame, no title, and no
way to move it.

Report frame margins for top-level dialogs (Qt::Dialog windows that are
not frameless, not maximized or full screen, and not customized without
a title): a title strip 1.75 lines of the application font tall, at
least 30 dp, and a 1 dp border.  QtWindow is given the margins, the
title and colours from the application palette, makes its view larger
than the client area by the margins, and draws the strip and the border
in them.  Dragging the strip moves the window; QtWindow also moves, and
if need be shrinks, a decorated window to keep it between the system
bars, as a window manager would.  It reports the new client rect through
decorationGeometry().  The cross in the strip sends a close event through
decorationClose().

The client area stays the window's geometry, so frameGeometry(), pos()
and move() of widgets include the frame as on desktop.  A position that
includes the frame (WindowFrameInclusive) is shifted by the margins
before it reaches QtWindow.

QT_ANDROID_NO_WINDOW_DECORATION=1 turns the frames off.

The Java side is in KiCad Android's QtWindow.java replacement
(android/qt-patches), as this tree's jar is not rebuilt.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
